from curriculum_library import dossier

dossier(41,'书信：服务投诉','A disputed repair bill',
'The estimate~A bicycle shop estimated eighty pounds to replace a wheel. The customer approved that work by email. The estimate stated that additional work required separate permission.||The final invoice~At collection, the bill included a new chain and brake cables. The mechanic said they improved safety, but could not show that the customer had authorized them.||Separating issues~The customer did not dispute the wheel replacement. Her complaint concerned work beyond the agreed scope and the absence of a call explaining the extra cost.||A proposed resolution~She requested an itemized bill and a written explanation. She was willing to discuss necessary repairs but did not accept that a general concern about safety automatically authorized every charge.',
'A complaint opening~Please review invoice 418 for the repair completed on 12 May. I agreed to the wheel replacement described in your estimate, but the final charge includes two additional items.||Supporting evidence~I have attached the estimate and my acceptance email. Neither document authorizes a chain replacement. I received no request for approval before collecting the bicycle.||The requested action~Please explain how the additional work was approved and correct any unauthorized charges. I would appreciate a written response within ten working days.||An appropriate tone~I value the work your shop has done previously and hope we can resolve this directly. My request concerns this invoice; I am not claiming that every repair was unnecessary.',
'The first response~Adviser: I can see the estimate, but the mechanic is away today. May I call you tomorrow after checking the job sheet? Customer: Yes, after three.||The disputed amount~Adviser: Are you questioning the whole invoice? Customer: No, only the chain and cable charges that were added after the estimate.||Evidence requested~Adviser: Please forward the acceptance email. Customer: I will include the invoice number in the subject line so you can match it.||Next steps~Adviser: I will not promise a refund before checking, but I will send a written explanation by Friday.',
'A cleaning agreement~A cleaning company quotes separately for ordinary rooms and specialist stain removal. Customers choose additional services when approving the booking.||Unexpected work~If staff find a stain requiring specialist treatment, they must contact the customer before proceeding. Leaving a key does not authorize extra charges.||A cancellation clause~Visits cancelled less than twenty-four hours ahead incur a call-out fee unless the company agrees otherwise. The fee is distinct from payment for completed cleaning.||A complaint channel~Customers should report disputed charges with the booking reference and photographs where relevant. The company aims to acknowledge complaints within two working days.')

dossier(42,'书信：信息咨询','Before joining a course',
'An appealing description~A photography course promises practical instruction for beginners. The advertisement gives a total fee but does not explain whether students need their own camera or pay separately for printing.||A learner\'s constraints~The applicant works alternate Saturdays and owns only a phone camera. These circumstances could affect attendance and equipment requirements even if the course level is suitable.||Questions worth asking~She asks about required equipment, missed-session arrangements and the proportion of time spent practising. A course called practical may still contain substantial classroom explanation.||Waiting for clarity~She requests written details before paying. This is not an application for a refund; no booking has yet been made. Clear information helps both the provider and learner avoid an unsuitable purchase.',
'A precise enquiry~I am interested in your introductory photography course starting in June. Before enrolling, I would like to clarify the equipment requirements and attendance arrangements.||Explaining relevant circumstances~I currently use a phone camera and work on alternate Saturdays. Please let me know whether a dedicated camera is essential and whether missed sessions can be attended with another group.||Clarifying costs~Does the advertised fee include printing and any admission charges for field visits? If additional expenses are likely, an approximate amount would help me plan.||A clear closing~Please also send the full timetable and cancellation conditions. I will decide whether to enroll after reviewing these details. Thank you for helping me assess whether the course is suitable.',
'The equipment answer~Tutor: A camera with manual controls is needed after the second lesson. We have four loan cameras, but they must be reserved.||The timetable answer~Learner: Could I attend a different group on the Saturdays I work? Tutor: There is a Wednesday group, subject to space. We cannot guarantee every replacement session.||Additional costs~Tutor: The fee includes classroom materials. Students pay their own travel costs for the final outdoor assignment.||Making a reservation~Learner: Please send the available loan dates first. I will not confirm the course until I know I can borrow a camera.',
'A language retreat~A weekend language retreat includes accommodation and group meals. Travel to the venue is not included. Participants arrange their own arrival.||Room choices~The advertised rate is for a shared room. Single rooms carry a supplement and are limited. A preference entered on the form is not confirmation of availability.||Teaching arrangements~Morning classes are followed by optional afternoon activities. Individual tuition can be booked separately, but is not part of the standard package.||Before payment~Applicants receive a written offer showing the final room and price. They should check that offer before paying the deposit.')

dossier(43,'书信：变更安排','Rescheduling a delivery',
'The booking~A furniture delivery was arranged for Friday between eight and twelve. The customer later learned that essential building maintenance would prevent lift access during that period.||The practical difficulty~The order included a large wardrobe. Carrying it up five floors was neither part of the agreed service nor a safe assumption for the customer to make.||A proposed alternative~The customer requested Monday afternoon, when the lift would be available again. She supplied the order number and asked whether changing the date would incur a fee.||Written confirmation~Until the supplier confirmed, the original booking remained in place. Sending a request did not by itself cancel or amend the delivery arrangement.',
'Explaining the change~I need to request a new delivery date for order 762. Building management has scheduled lift maintenance during the Friday morning slot we agreed.||Providing workable options~Monday after one or Tuesday before noon would be suitable. Please confirm whether either period is available and whether your team needs access measurements in advance.||Avoiding assumptions~I understand that a change may affect your route planning. Please tell me about any additional charge before updating the booking, rather than adding it after delivery.||Confirming the outcome~Once we agree on a new slot, please send an updated confirmation showing the date, access instructions and contact number for the driver.',
'A supplier call~Scheduler: Monday afternoon is full, but Tuesday morning is available. Customer: That works if the team arrives before noon.||A time limit~Scheduler: The window is nine to twelve. We cannot promise an exact minute, but the driver calls thirty minutes ahead.||A charge~Customer: Is there a change fee? Scheduler: No, because you contacted us more than two working days in advance.||Confirmation~Customer: Please replace Friday with Tuesday and send the details. Scheduler: I will email confirmation now; keep the original order number.',
'A removal notice~A moving company allows one date change without an administration fee if requested at least five working days before the move. Availability is still required.||Changes in volume~Adding furniture may alter the quotation even when the date-change fee is waived. Customers should update the inventory rather than assume the original vehicle is large enough.||Access restrictions~Customers must report parking or lift restrictions before moving day. The company cannot arrange building permission on their behalf.||Final confirmation~The revised date becomes binding only when the company issues an updated booking confirmation. A telephone enquiry alone does not hold the requested day.')

dossier(44,'书信：建议与请求','Improving a shared workspace',
'A recurring interruption~Workers in a shared office struggled to hold confidential calls. Meeting rooms were often booked for larger groups, and ordinary desks offered little privacy.||An affordable proposal~A tenant suggested converting a small storage room into a call booth. The proposal required ventilation, sound treatment and a booking rule, not merely moving in a chair.||Considering other users~The storage room contained supplies used by cleaners. Those supplies would need a suitable new location. Improving one service should not make another team\'s work impractical.||A trial before expansion~Management proposed one booth first, with limits on booking length. Usage and complaints would guide any later investment in additional booths.',
'Stating the issue~I am writing on behalf of several tenants about the difficulty of finding a private place for short calls. The problem is most noticeable between ten and twelve.||Describing the effect~Some tenants postpone client calls, while others speak in the corridor. Neither option provides a reliable or appropriate setting for confidential discussions.||Offering a practical suggestion~Could management assess whether the small storage room could become a ventilated call booth? Please consult the cleaning team about alternative storage before making changes.||Requesting a next step~We would welcome a short trial with a booking limit. Please let us know whether an assessment is possible and how tenants can contribute feedback.',
'A management discussion~Manager: The storage room can be adapted, but ventilation will cost more than the furniture. Tenant: Could we test demand before committing?||A temporary measure~Manager: We can reserve a small meeting room for short calls next week. It will not be fully soundproof, so users must understand that limitation.||Booking rules~Tenant: What booking length do you suggest? Manager: Twenty minutes initially, with no consecutive reservations during busy hours.||Feedback~Manager: Record unsuccessful booking attempts as well as completed calls. Otherwise we may underestimate demand.',
'A shared-kitchen proposal~An office plans to add a second microwave. Staff have reported long lunchtime queues, but electrical capacity must be checked before installation.||A temporary arrangement~Teams may use staggered lunch periods voluntarily. This is a temporary option, not a permanent change to contractual working hours.||Cleaning responsibility~Users must wipe spills immediately. A weekly cleaning rota supplements this requirement rather than replacing it.||Evaluation~Management will compare waiting times before and after installation. Purchasing another appliance will not count as success if the queue remains unchanged.')

dossier(45,'议论：公共交通','Should fares be lower?',
'The argument for access~Lower bus fares can make work, education and services more affordable. This benefit may be especially important for people without cars, although the effect depends on where and when buses operate.||The capacity question~If services are already overcrowded, lower fares may increase demand without improving journeys. Passengers who cannot board gain little from a cheaper ticket. Frequency and reliability therefore matter alongside price.||Funding tradeoffs~Lost ticket income must be replaced or operating costs reduced. A policy funded by cutting less-used routes could disadvantage the very communities it aims to help.||A qualified approach~Targeted discounts combined with service improvements may be more practical than a universal reduction. The best choice requires local information about costs, demand and unmet need.',
'A position with a condition~I support reduced fares where cost prevents essential travel, but fare policy should be considered with service quality. An inexpensive service that rarely runs cannot provide dependable access.||Developing the first reason~Workers on low incomes may spend a significant share of earnings on commuting. Discounts can reduce this burden and expand the range of jobs they can realistically reach.||Considering an objection~Critics argue that fare reductions divert money from maintenance. This concern is reasonable if no replacement funding exists. A proposal should therefore identify its funding source rather than assume discounts pay for themselves.||A focused conclusion~Authorities should assess whether price or availability is the main barrier in each area. Investment can then address the actual constraint instead of applying the same measure everywhere.',
'A policy discussion~Councillor: We can fund either a fare discount or an extra evening service this year. Analyst: Which problem do residents report most often?||Evidence~Councillor: Shift workers say the last bus leaves too early. Analyst: A discount would not solve that. We should estimate demand for a later departure.||An additional concern~Councillor: Some daytime users also struggle with fares. Analyst: A targeted concession might be possible, but it needs separate costing.||A decision boundary~Councillor: Prepare both costs and explain who benefits. We should not present one policy as solving every transport problem.',
'A rail concession~A regional rail pass offers discounted off-peak journeys to registered residents. It cannot be used on services marked as peak departures.||Eligibility~Proof of residence is required each year. Owning a property in the region without living there does not satisfy the published requirement.||Reservations~The pass reduces the fare but does not reserve a seat. Passengers must make a separate reservation on services where one is compulsory.||A review~The operator will examine journey patterns and revenue after twelve months. The concession is a trial rather than a permanent entitlement.')

dossier(46,'议论：技术与学习','What an app can and cannot do',
'Immediate practice~Learning apps can provide short exercises whenever time is available. They can also return feedback quickly. These features help practice happen regularly, but do not establish that every type of feedback is accurate.||A measurement problem~Points and streaks usually measure activity within the app. A learner may maintain a long streak while repeatedly choosing familiar exercises. Independent performance on unfamiliar tasks is a stronger test of transfer.||The role of explanation~An incorrect answer needs more than a red mark. Learners benefit from seeing why an answer is wrong and then applying the explanation in a different context. Automatically displaying the solution may encourage passive reading.||A combined approach~Apps are most useful when practice, meaningful output and external checks support each other. They should make weaknesses visible rather than promise that completing a sequence guarantees an examination score.',
'A learner sets boundaries~I use an app for brief listening and vocabulary practice, but reserve time for speaking and writing. Otherwise, I naturally choose tasks where I already feel successful.||Checking feedback~When AI corrects a sentence, I ask for an explanation tied to my meaning. If two suggested corrections conflict, I compare examples and avoid memorizing a rule I do not understand.||Preserving evidence~I save original recordings and drafts alongside revised versions. Looking only at a polished final answer can hide how much assistance was required to produce it.||A useful test~Every few weeks I attempt an unfamiliar task without hints. The result may be less comfortable than my app score, but it helps identify what I can actually do independently.',
'A coaching exchange~Learner: My practice score is ninety percent. Does that mean I have IELTS 6.5? Coach: No. What tasks produced that score, and did you use hints?||A clarification~Learner: They were short multiple-choice readings I had seen before. Coach: That shows familiarity with those tasks, not performance across four examination skills.||A next step~Coach: Try a new reading under appropriate conditions and record a spontaneous response. Keep the results separate from repeated practice.||Feedback limits~Coach: I can help analyze your work, but an AI estimate must remain labelled as an estimate rather than an official result.',
'An app subscription notice~A language app offers a free trial with access to selected lessons. Advanced feedback features are excluded from the trial and are listed separately.||Progress records~Completed lessons synchronize only when the user signs into the same account. Offline activity may not appear on another device until a connection is restored.||Cancellation~Deleting the app does not necessarily cancel a subscription purchased through an app store. Users should follow the billing provider\'s cancellation process.||Score claims~The app\'s internal levels are learning categories. They are not examination certificates or guaranteed equivalents of an external test score.')

dossier(47,'议论：工作培训','Who should pay for training?',
'Employer-specific skills~Training required to use a company\'s own system directly supports its operations. Expecting new staff to pay for such instruction may create unfair barriers and discourage questions during early learning.||Transferable benefits~Other courses develop qualifications useful across many employers. Both the worker and organization may benefit. Shared funding can be reasonable, but the terms should be clear before the employee enrolls.||Time as a cost~Even a fully paid course requires time. Evening attendance may conflict with caring duties or rest. A policy that covers fees while ignoring time can still exclude some employees.||Retention without pressure~Employers may worry that trained workers will leave. Positive working conditions and opportunities to use new skills can support retention without relying entirely on restrictive repayment conditions.',
'A request for support~I would like to discuss funding for a spreadsheet course relevant to our monthly reporting process. The course covers data checking and clearer presentation, areas where our team currently spends considerable time.||A concrete benefit~After completing it, I propose revising one recurring report and sharing the method with colleagues. This would provide a practical way to assess whether the training improves our work.||The resources needed~The fee is listed in the attached course information. I also request two afternoons of study time. Please clarify whether any repayment conditions would apply before I register.||A realistic commitment~I cannot promise a particular financial saving in advance. I can, however, document the revised process and compare the time and errors involved before and after the change.',
'A manager responds~Manager: We can pay the course fee, but the team is busy on Fridays. Employee: There is a Tuesday afternoon group covering the same material.||Checking equivalence~Manager: Does it include the practical assessment? Employee: Yes, and the provider confirms that both groups lead to the same certificate.||Sharing learning~Manager: Please prepare a short demonstration afterwards. Employee: I can show the revised report, but colleagues will still need practice themselves.||Written terms~Manager: I will send the approval and study-time arrangement before you book.',
'A training allowance~Employees may apply for an annual development allowance. Approval depends on relevance to an agreed learning objective and the available budget.||Prior approval~Courses booked before approval may not be reimbursed. A receipt proves payment but does not replace permission under the policy.||Completion evidence~Applicants must provide attendance or completion evidence. Examination success is required only where the approval explicitly states that condition.||Unused funds~The allowance does not accumulate automatically from one year to the next. Staff should check the current policy before planning a more expensive future course.')

dossier(48,'议论：城市与住房','Building homes near transport',
'A planning proposal~A city proposed new apartments beside a railway station. Supporters argued that residents could make fewer car journeys. This depended on reliable services and the locations of jobs and essential facilities.||Pressure on services~More residents would also need schools, healthcare and public space. Housing numbers alone could not show whether the neighborhood would function well. Service capacity needed assessment alongside construction plans.||Affordability concerns~Convenient locations often attract higher prices. Without attention to affordability, the people most dependent on public transport might be unable to live near it.||Balancing objectives~A strong plan combines housing supply, access and public services. No single feature, such as proximity to a station, guarantees a successful development in isolation.',
'A response to consultation~I support additional housing near the station, but the proposal needs clearer information about pedestrian access. The current crossing is difficult during the morning rush.||Using local evidence~Residents walking from the north side must make a long detour to reach the entrance. A shorter safe crossing could benefit existing residents as well as those in the new apartments.||Avoiding an unrelated objection~My concern is not that new residents should be kept out. It is that transport access should be improved as part of the development rather than left as an uncertain future project.||Requesting a commitment~Please publish the planned crossing improvements, funding and timetable before final approval. This would make it possible to judge whether the access benefits claimed in the proposal are realistic.',
'A planning hearing~Resident: The report says the station is five minutes away. Was that measured from the site entrance or the nearest corner? Planner: From the nearest corner.||A relevant distinction~Resident: The public entrance is on the opposite side. People will have to walk farther. Planner: We should show the actual pedestrian route.||An improvement~Resident: Could a new gate shorten it? Planner: Possibly, but it would require agreement from the neighboring landowner.||A revised statement~Planner: Until that agreement exists, the report should use the current route rather than assume the gate will be built.',
'A housing register~Applicants to an affordable-rent scheme must provide current income and household information. Joining the register does not guarantee an immediate offer.||Changes~Households should report significant changes in circumstances. An old application may no longer reflect eligibility or housing needs.||Viewing offers~Applicants invited to view a property receive a response deadline. Failing to respond may allow the offer to pass to another household.||Additional charges~The advertised rent may exclude utilities and specified service charges. Applicants should check the full cost before accepting an offer.')

dossier(49,'议论：旅游与社区','Visitors and local life',
'Economic opportunities~Visitors support accommodation, restaurants and cultural attractions. For some communities this income sustains jobs that would otherwise be scarce. However, benefits may be unevenly distributed among residents.||Pressure on shared places~Popular streets and public transport can become crowded at peak times. Problems may be concentrated in a few locations rather than spread evenly across the whole area.||Managing timing~Promoting visits outside the busiest season can spread demand, but it depends on weather, opening hours and transport. Advertising alone cannot create an attractive off-season experience.||A community role~Residents should have a voice in managing public spaces and visitor behavior. A successful tourism policy considers daily life alongside visitor numbers and spending.',
'A museum director explains~We introduced timed entry after queues blocked the pavement. Tickets did not become more expensive, but visitors had to choose a slot. The purpose was to distribute arrivals rather than reduce access permanently.||An unintended barrier~Some visitors arrived without internet access and assumed they could no longer enter. We kept a small allocation for same-day counter bookings and improved signs outside.||Working with neighbors~Nearby shops preferred predictable visitor flows to large queues outside their doors. We coordinated event times so several attractions did not release crowds onto the same street simultaneously.||Reviewing the change~We tracked waiting times and refused entry as well as total admissions. Shorter queues would not represent success if many people were simply unable to obtain tickets.',
'A guide briefing~Guide: The old market is busy today. Keep the group beside the fountain while I explain the history, and leave the shop entrances clear.||A visitor question~Visitor: Can we photograph the stalls? Guide: Ask the stallholder first, especially when people are clearly visible. Some are happy to agree; others prefer not to.||A schedule change~Guide: The church is holding a service, so we will visit the garden first. The total tour length remains ninety minutes.||A return point~Guide: We finish at the station, not back here. Tell me now if you need directions to the original meeting point afterwards.',
'A coastal shuttle~A summer shuttle connects the railway station with three beaches. Tickets allow unlimited travel on the chosen day but are not valid on ordinary town buses.||Seasonal operation~The service runs daily in July and August and only at weekends in June. Visitors should check the calendar before relying on a weekday June journey.||Accessibility~One wheelchair space is available on each vehicle. Groups needing several spaces should contact the operator about alternatives in advance.||Weather disruption~Severe weather may close coastal roads. Unused day tickets can be refunded when the operator cancels the entire service for that day.')

dossier(50,'阶段检查：观点与行动','A town-center experiment',
'The proposal~A town closed one shopping street to ordinary traffic on selected Saturdays. Emergency access and early deliveries remained possible. The experiment was more limited than a permanent ban on vehicles throughout the center.||Different expectations~Some traders expected more pedestrians, while others feared losing customers carrying heavy purchases. Both predictions needed testing. Opinions before the trial could not substitute for observations during it.||Collecting useful evidence~The council compared footfall, sales reports and access complaints with similar Saturdays. Weather and special events were recorded because they could influence the results independently of the closure.||A possible revision~Early results suggested longer visits but difficulties with collecting large items. A designated collection point was proposed. The trial could be improved without treating every problem as a reason to abandon it.',
'A trader\'s balanced view~My cafe gained customers during the trial, but the furniture shop next door faced collection problems. My positive experience did not prove that every business benefited equally.||A practical adjustment~We suggested a short loading period late in the afternoon and a clear collection point. Deliveries could then occur without reopening the street to unrestricted traffic all day.||A wider measure~Sales matter, but so do access for disabled visitors and safety for children. The council should publish these measures together rather than selecting only the results that support a preferred policy.||A conditional recommendation~I support extending the trial after addressing collection access. Permanent adoption should depend on repeated evidence across different seasons, not only a successful summer weekend.',
'The review meeting~Officer: Pedestrian counts rose, but three shops reported lower sales. Trader: Were those shops affected by building work as well?||Checking causes~Officer: Two were. We should separate that information before attributing every change to the traffic trial.||An access issue~Resident: The temporary drop-off point was poorly signed. Officer: We can improve signs before the next trial day.||The decision~Chair: Continue the trial for another month with the revised access arrangement. We will not announce permanent adoption until the review is complete.',
'A market relocation~A weekly market is moving temporarily while drainage work takes place. The new site is behind the sports hall, with stalls retaining their usual opening hours.||Transport~A free shuttle runs from the old site every thirty minutes. It carries passengers, but cannot transport large purchases such as furniture.||Trader arrangements~Stallholders receive their new positions by email. Positions are provisional because access routes must remain clear for emergency vehicles.||Returning~The council expects work to finish in six weeks, but will confirm the return date after inspection. The market will not move back solely because six weeks have elapsed.')
